From a51ea49d5888420f8073dd1723ac9e0d4a582de2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:02:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] SECURITY-D3: a write must be addressed to a host this app
 answers to

The same-origin check counted the request's own Host as "here", and under
DNS rebinding the Host is the attacker's: a page on evil.example re-points
its name at this machine and posts to evil.example, so Origin and Host
agree. On a fresh LAN install with LOCAL_LOGIN that post could create the
first member through /auth/first-run before the owner had set it up.

Unsafe methods now need a Host (or, behind TRUST_PROXY, the right-most
X-Forwarded-Host) that is BASE_URL's hostname with its port or none, or an
IP literal or localhost - names no DNS answer is involved in, so they
cannot be rebound, and the LAN address a household types keeps working.
Anything else is 403 before the Origin comparison. Bearer requests and
reads are unchanged.

Operator-visible: a write addressed by any other DNS name (a second
hostname, a proxy that rewrites Host) is now refused; BASE_URL must name
the host the browser uses. Documented in operations.md and security.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
